  PRE-REGISTERED SIMPLE / COMPOSED SPLIT, on the single-optimal subset only
  (where there is no tie-break to confuse the reading):
    SIMPLE   (0-1 own threat)  n=  528  chance 0.2547   tree 0.6685   linear 0.5509
                                     lift over chance:  tree  2.62x   linear  2.16x
    COMPOSED (2+ own threats)  n=  718  chance 0.5395   tree 0.8008   linear 0.6435
                                     lift over chance:  tree  1.48x   linear  1.19x

  *** THE HEADROOM CORRECTION, AND WHY THE NAIVE READING IS WRONG ***

  Raw accuracy says COMPOSED is the HARD subset for the model. Raw accuracy is
  confounded, and the confound is visible in the chance line above:
    SIMPLE   chance 0.2547  (mean ~3.9 empty cells -- early game)
    COMPOSED chance 0.5395  (mean ~1.9 empty cells -- LATE game)

  A position with two of your own lines one stone from completing is, structurally,
  a LATE position. Late positions have fewer empty cells, so chance is already high,
  so a fixed amount of model skill moves the number less. Comparing raw accuracy
  across subsets with different chance levels compares the chance levels, not the
  models.

  The right normalisation is FRACTION OF HEADROOM: how much of the gap between
  chance and 1.0 the model actually closes.

    subset                       chance     tree   linear     tree fills  linear fills
    SIMPLE   (0-1 own threat)    0.2547   0.6685   0.5509          55.5%         39.7%
    COMPOSED (2+ own threats)    0.5395   0.8008   0.6435          56.7%         22.6%

  THE RESULT, AS MEASURED:

    decision tree: 55.5% of headroom on SIMPLE, 56.7% on COMPOSED. INDISTINGUISHABLE.
                    The non-linear model shows NO composition penalty at all.
    linear 9x9:    39.7% of headroom on SIMPLE, 22.6% on COMPOSED. A REAL DROP,
                    roughly a 43% loss of closed headroom.

  So the pre-registered prediction is HALF right, and the half that survives is the
  more specific one. The COMPOSITION PENALTY IS REAL FOR THE LINEAR MODEL AND ABSENT
  FOR THE TREE. That is a sharper claim than 'composition is hard': it says the
  penalty is a property of ADDITIVITY, not of the task. A sum of local votes cannot
  represent a count over separate lines; a model that can form intermediate
  conjunctions can, and does. Minimax composition is not beyond these models -- it is
  beyond THIS representation.

  This also retro-explains the original 0.1807. That number was never 'a neural net
  is bad at minimax'. It was 'a linear map is bad at minimax', which is true and much
  less interesting, and the number was reported without the ceiling that would have
  said so.

  WHAT THIS DOES SUPPORT: a depth-matched COMPOSED-vs-SIMPLE contrast, which is now
  the obvious next experiment, and the reason it is worth running is that the naive
  version of it produces the OPPOSITE sign.
